\description{
\code{creel_connect_api()} creates a \code{creel_connection} object that fetches data
from a REST API returning JSON arrays.

Requests follow the pattern:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{GET \{base_url\}/\{endpoint\}?\{uid_param\}=\{uid1,uid2,...\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
\subsection{No API is assumed}{

The package ships no endpoint paths and no field names for any organisation's
API: both \code{endpoints} and \code{api_field_map} are required, and the call aborts
without them. A creel API's paths and JSON keys are properties of that
deployment, not of this package, and a built-in default would quietly decode
one agency's payload while misreading everyone else's.

Keep the two together in a YAML profile outside your analysis code and load
it with \code{\link[=creel_connect_from_yaml]{creel_connect_from_yaml()}}. A commented template ships with the
package:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{system.file("extdata", "api-profile-example.yml", package = "tidycreel.connect")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Requests are read-only}{

Every request this connection makes is a \code{GET}. Nothing is posted, patched
or deleted, and no file is written locally, so pointing it at a production
service cannot modify anything there.
}

\subsection{Failed requests}{

Every request is retried up to three times on a status that suggests the
failure is temporary: 408, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503 and 504. Retrying is safe
because every request is a \code{GET}, so a repeat cannot duplicate a side effect.
A \code{Retry-After} header is honoured, so a throttled request waits the interval
the server asked for rather than a fixed backoff.

Any other status at or above 400 aborts on the first response, carrying the
status, the endpoint and the body: a 401 or a 404 will give the same answer
three times, and retrying only delays it.

A response that returns 200 while carrying an error document rather than
records is also refused, quoting what the API said. Nothing is wrong at the
HTTP layer there, so the alternative is reading the error object as a record
and failing later with a message that blames \code{api_field_map} for a fault in
the request.
}

\subsection{Authentication}{

Three auth modes are supported via the \code{auth} argument:
\itemize{
\item \code{NULL} -- no authentication (default)
\item \code{list(type = "bearer", token = "...")} -- \code{Authorization: Bearer} header
\item \code{list(type = "api_key", key = "...", header = "X-API-Key")} -- arbitrary
header name (defaults to \code{"X-API-Key"} if \code{header} is omitted)
}

Credentials should be read from environment variables rather than stored
as plain strings:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{auth = list(type = "bearer", token = Sys.getenv("CREEL_API_TOKEN"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}
}
